{ignore}
<p><code>{tab}</code> is the item of the seven <a href="?tag/widget">widgets</a> that take a list
of labelled parts: <code>{tabs}</code> (a tab and its panel), <code>{accordion}</code> (a
<code>&lt;details&gt;</code> with the label as its summary), <code>{carousel}</code> (a slide
with the label as its caption), <code>{wizard}</code> (a step), <code>{lightbox}</code> (a
picture with its caption), <code>{splitpane}</code> (one of its two panes) and
<code>{sortable}</code> (an item put in order, the label its key). It prints nothing where it stands: its content is rendered, trimmed,
and handed to the nearest of those owners above it, which builds its markup from the items once
its own content has rendered.</p>

<p>Because the hand-over goes to the nearest owner and not to the direct parent, a tab may
stand inside an <code>{if}</code>, or inside a loop that makes a tab per row of data:
<code>{tabs}{plans}{tab $name}...{/tab}{/plans}{/tabs}</code>. The owners are widgets, reached
by their bare names after every other kind of tag; <code>{tab}</code> is a built-in tag, so a row
with a field named <code>tab</code> still makes tabs.</p>

<p>Under the strict check a <code>{tab}</code> outside any owner is an error, as is one that
never closes, and a tab of a <code>{tabs}</code>, an <code>{accordion}</code>, a
<code>{wizard}</code> or a <code>{splitpane}</code> without a label, an item of a
<code>{sortable}</code> without its key. A carousel slide and a lightbox picture may go without a
label; they then have no caption.</p>
{/ignore}
